  "interview": {
    "expected_score": 30,
    "expected_score_probability": 50,
    "full_score_probability": 3,
    "hardest_part": "Converting a static screenshot into a correct 3D read: judging ledge distances, which surfaces are actually climbable, and where the route continues once the camera is jammed against a cliff. Scoring is one point per vertical metre, so the real skill is sustained navigation through several regions rather than any single jump.",
    "likely_failure": "A mis-timed leap or a short fall on an exposed face, followed by stamina drain or a status effect (hunger, poison, cold) with no clear on-screen cue in the frame. Silent deaths where I cannot tell whether I am falling, out of stamina, or just facing the wrong way are the most probable loss points.",
    "helpful_mechanics": "Very fine-grained scoring — 100 separate one-metre milestones means partial progress still earns points, so even a short expedition scores. Held-key inputs latch for a full second, which suits coarse movement, and the deterministic arrow-key/J-K aliases remove mouse-accuracy dependence for turning and climbing.",
    "harmful_mechanics": "One screenshot per decision while the game keeps running in real time — I act on stale frames and cannot react to a slip. Free-look control is slow (600 px/s aliases) and imprecise for lining up jumps, one attempt with no retries punishes any single mistake, and I cannot read the game's audio or status icons reliably from a still image."
  }
